welcome everyone and thank you for  joining us for today's session on  pioneering our understanding of the  human brain I'd like to introduce dr.  Teresa Cruz she is a health scientist  administrator who manages grants in the  devices and technology development and  rehabilitation diagnostics and  interventions programs at the National  Institute for medical rehabilitation and  research within the National Institute  for Child Health and Human Development  dr. crews will be replacing dr. Nick  Lionel's our original presenter can  begin the track on innovative new  technologies she will be presenting on  NIH funding for technology development  and translations to humans my name is  dr. Rebecca Shannon and I will be your  moderator for today's event  we are delighted to bring this  educational web seminar presented by  legumes we would like to extend our  special thanks to the brain initiative  program directors in particular dr.  James mad for their efforts in  organizing today's session before we  begin I would like to remind everyone  that this event is interactive we  encourage you to participate by  submitting your questions during the  presentation simply click on the ask a  question box located on the far left of  your screen and type your questions in  the drop-down box our speaker will  respond to your questions by an email  following the presentation this  presentation is educational and this  offers free continuing education credits  please click on the continuing education  grants tab located at the top right  corner of the presentation window and  all the process to obtain your credits  now without further ado please join me  in welcoming dr. Teresa Cruz hello thank  you for the invitation to speak today  about NIH funding opportunities for  technology development for translation  to humans again my name is Teresa Cruz  and I'm a program officer at the NIH  earlier in this  conference you heard a description of  the brain initiative from a couple of my  colleagues so I'm not going to talk  about the whole of brain initiative now  but I would like to bring your attention  to our device funding pipeline at the  early stage we have a new concepts RFA  and this is really for early stage ideas  where you don't even have preliminary  data yet but you're looking at  developing technologies for invasive  recording and stimulation of the central  nervous system and this is an r21 we  move down the pipeline to the ro1  mechanism which is what we refer to as  our new tools RFA and this allows for  larger applications  larger budgets to really test out those  new ideas from the r21 in in vivo  settings  following on our new technologies ro1 we  have the brain device optimization uo1  which is a cooperative agreement and  here we're really thinking about how do  we get these new technologies into the  hands of the neuroscience community and  what do we need to do to get that  technology to be ready for use in labs  or in some cases in human subjects these  three do not require that the ultimate  user be a clinical clinical setting but  several of them are  the next part of the pipeline is for  human devices and here we have two  different flavors one is a phased award  and there's two different types here and  then we have a direct clinical trial of  one  so to zoom in on these a little bit the  purpose here is to support development  optimization and translational  activities and small clinical studies  involving therapeutic and diagnostic  devices for disorders that affect the  nervous system or the musculoskeletal  system  so for our phased awards this is the ug  3 and the uh3 or the SBIR u 44 phase 1  phase 2  combined FastTrack award and for these  RFA is there are some device development  that needs to occur and that's in the  either phase one for the fast-track SBIR  which is for small businesses or in the  ug three phase which is for academic  institutions and here you're going to be  doing your pre IDE studies your enabling  work for the FDA  after that's completed and an IRB has  been approved then you can move into the  early feasibility studies and our first  in human clinical trials  it's possible that a technology is ready  for that at this point and in that case  the applicant can go them directly into  the uh3 brain next-generation devices  RFA  so a couple things to note about these  funding opportunities for translation so  the first so that they're all  cooperative agreements there are not  grant mechanisms so that means that  there is significant involvement on the  behalf of NIH staff in the scientific  development and execution of these  awards  this is particularly related to the  milestone development so much of NIH  funds funding goes to hypothesis based  research or discovery driven research  these are different in that they are a  heavily milestone and they are device  development so there is a bit of a  difference here between traditional NIH  Awards you need to be working on  invasive tech devices you need to be  tapping into the central nervous system  which also includes the spinal cord and  the retina in addition to the brain  these are all considered clinical trials  so there is some intervention on behalf  of the device you may need to be  prepared with your clinical trial  information when you submit to NIH so  you cannot have a delayed onset and  there are some budget arrey requirements  these are typically larger budgets but  we do have caps particularly for the  small business awards we have due dates  three times a year and our next due date  is in June so  one of the nice things about this  translational program is that they many  of the applicants tap into the NIH brain  public-private partnership program this  is not required but we found that it's  been very helpful to many of our  applicants so this PPP program was  developed between NIH and several device  manufacturers and the hope was to  facilitate partnerships between the  individual investigators at academic  institutions and the device  manufacturers so in this program and I  encourage you to visit our website the  company's several of them are listed  here  provide technical support and  information about their neuro  stimulation devices  many of them have capabilities that are  beyond what is typically used in the  clinic NIH has also arranged for pre  negotiated agreement templates that can  help facilitate those relationships  between the investigator and the  manufacturer so each group doesn't have  to start from scratch when they work on  their  intellectual and material property  agreements  so now I'm going to give a few examples  of grants that have cooperative  agreements that have been funded through  the translational programs of the brain  initiative so the first is from AI  shotgun does and Kelly foot at the  University of Florida  they are working on a closed-loop  control of a central tremor treatment  this is a deep brain stimulation and in  the video that you'll see in just a  couple seconds you'll see a person who  been  who has the device installed and you'll  see that when the stimulator is off he  tremors quite considerably when the  stimulator is turned on you can see the  signal that goes into the DBS system to  turn on the stimulator and that signal  is indicative of the beginning of  movement so if you like to play that  video now  [Music]  you  okay so I hope you noticed  that this is an example of the  public-private partnership between  University of Florida and  Medtronic for the implanted stimulator  and of course the benefit of having a  closed loop control where the stimulator  is only turned on when the patient is  initiating movement is a preservation of  battery life and a reduction in  side-effects from having the DBS on  constantly so another example of the  translational program is from Phil troit  who is the PI  as part of a large group out of  iit and he and his colleagues are  working on a visual prosthetic and in  this case the  stimulation is  directed on to the  visual cortex so the signal comes in  through a camera it is processed and  then  wirelessly transmitted to the micro  electrode array which is  installed in the visual cortex they are  working on their pre ide work at the  moment in an animal model a canine model  and they hope to  recruit five human subjects for this  study as it proceeds and our third  example is from Greg Worrell at the Mayo  Clinic  he is also working in a canine model but  here they are working on an epilepsy  therapeutic platform whereby you can  measure oncoming seizures with implanted  EEG that gets processed  and then stimulation can be administered  in a closed-loop action to reduce the  focal epilepsy  again we are in k9 models but are  planning to move into  clinical trials in the upcoming grant  periods  it's just like to take a moment to  acknowledge another NIH initiative as  related to device development for  stimulation and recording of the central  nervous system and that is the heal  initiative which stands for helping and  addiction long-term there are many  facets to it but one of them is this  again device development pathway and  there are several opportunities  for applications to be submitted in  response to development of devices for  pain management and I would encourage  you to visit the NIH heal website to  look at those specific funding  opportunity  announcements so thank you for your time  today and enjoy your conference  thank you dr. Cruz for the informational  presentation as a reminder our speaker  will follow up with any questions about  her presentation by an email join us for  our next presentation in the innovative  neurotechnologies tract with our  presenters dr. Michael Gustav and dr.  John mcnutty who will present rathe our  analysis and visualization of  intracranial encephalography  enjoy - see you there
 

